From ceb37840c28725f48b1575317cc62a8bfb9002f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:53:01 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: capture process cores at exit and on request

Give the host the memory of the program it launched, as an ELF core the
kernel's own writer produces, without a core pattern, a helper or a
file in the guest.

At exit (EXEC flag CORE): the launched root process's core is written
into a retained in-kernel sink when the process dies, whatever ends it
(a signal, exit_group(), the last thread's exit). An armed root's fatal
signal goes through the kernel's core rendezvous instead of tearing the
group down at once, so every thread is in the core; SIGKILL keeps its
immediate teardown and then yields no core. On request (CORE_CAPTURE):
a live process is paused cooperatively, each thread parking at its next
return to user mode, written to a sink and resumed; CORE_LOOKUP and
CORE_CANCEL find and end such a capture.

The sink stores only the bytes written, in extents, so a core stays
sparse and holes in it (an untouched arena, a reserved heap) cost
nothing; CORE_READ names a hole instead of sending it. Its buffer grows
in 8 MiB chunks, and the size limit follows the guest's memory: half of
RAM less 128 MiB, clamped to 64 MiB .. 1 GiB, within a pool of twice
that. CORE_STATUS says why a core was cut (budget, memory, extent
table, logical size), CORE_CAPS reports the limits, and while a
finished core is being read the control worker polls at every tick.
A reattachment waits until no live capture holds parked threads, and
a run's Stop escalation waits for a core being copied, for at most 15 s
past its grace period.

Hooked kernel files:
- fs/coredump.c: do_coredump() hands an armed root's core to the sink,
  and two entry points let the sink drive the native writer at exit
  and for a live process (its own thread list, no core_state).
- fs/binfmt_elf.c and include/linux/coredump.h: the ELF writer emits
  into the sink, skips holes and honours the capture's deadline.
- kernel/exit.c: a group exit or the last thread's exit of an armed
  root starts the core, and a root that left without one publishes it
  as unavailable.
- kernel/signal.c: complete_signal() lets an armed root's fatal signal
  take the core path instead of the immediate group exit.

Contract: revision 15 (cores at exit, transport ABI 9) and revision 16
(live cores). Sparse reads, the sizing, CORE_CAPS and the stored-size
status are additive within revision 17.
